<article class="env-proposition" id="FE4679">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/FE4679">Proposition <span data-tag="FE4679">3.1.2.1</span>.</a>Cartesian fibration!and functor categories Let $p: X \rightarrow S$ be a Cartesian fibration of simplicial sets, and let $K$ be an arbitrary simplicial set. Then: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
The induced map $p^ K: X^{K} \rightarrow S^{K}$ is a Cartesian fibration. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
An edge $\Delta ^1 \rightarrow X^ K$ is $p^ K$-Cartesian if and only if, for every vertex $k$ of $K$, the induced edge $\Delta ^1 \rightarrow X$ is $p$-Cartesian. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/FE4679" data-tag="FE4679">3.1.2.1</a>.</strong>
       Since $p$ is a Cartesian fibration, it induces a map $X^{\natural } \rightarrow S^{\sharp }$ which has the right lifting property with respect to all marked anodyne maps. By Proposition <a href="/tag/B7A488" data-tag="B7A488">3.1.2.3</a>, the induced map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  (X^{\natural })^{K^{\flat }} \rightarrow (S^{\sharp })^{K^{\flat }}=(S^ K)^{\sharp } \]
</div>
    <p>
       has the right lifting property with respect to all marked anodyne morphisms. The desired result now follows from Remark <a href="/tag/A433BE" data-tag="A433BE">3.1.1.11</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>